{"id":"n-20261029-rhf2jb","category":"travel","display_title":"Greenstyle in Swiss Ski Resort","display_title_he":"גרינסטייל באתר הסקי השוויצרי","short_summary":"The Swiss ski resort of Laax is tackling the climate crisis head-on through innovative sustainability measures, aiming for carbon neutrality by 2030.","english_level":"C1","words":540,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/25/swiss-alps-ski-resort-laax-meeting-climate-crisis","title":"Carving out a greener future: how the Swiss ski resort of Laax is meeting the climate crisis head on","published_at":"2026-10-25T07:00:57.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-25T07:00:57.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-29T07:15:36.881Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["As lower-altitude European ski destinations grapple with diminishing snow and unpredictable winters, higher-elevation resorts are re-evaluating their futures. Parts of Europe previously accustomed to crisp snow fields and pristine blue skies now face conditions resembling a British winter, with shaggy yellow grass and slush under leaden skies. However, higher up, normal service continues. In the Swiss resort of Laax, where most slopes sit at about 2,000 metres, conditions remain snow-secure for now. Halfpipes buzz with snowboarders practicing tricks, and four-year-olds ski effortlessly.","Among them, Laax is taking an aggressive approach to environmental sustainability. Stung by climate crisis criticism levelled at the ski industry, the community is striving to achieve carbon neutrality by the year 2030. This ambitious goal involves cutting out all oil consumption, transitioning entirely to sustainable energy generation, and driving food waste down to zero. What is happening here may turn out to be the future of winter sports.","Reto Gurtner, the 71-year-old CEO of the Flims Laax resort whose father constructed the initial ski lifts in 1962, has long championed a visionary ethos known as 'greenstyle'. Gurtner recalls that Laax was once a poor village, though it had summer visitors since the 19th century. In the 1970s, Gurtner studied at UCLA and discovered skateboarding, bringing home a snowboard, a mountain bike, and an economics degree. Alongside local resistance—such as outfitters refusing to stock snowboards and farmers objecting to cyclists—he helped pioneer these new sports locally, building the first halfpipe on the glacier with shovels.","Today, this progressive mindset shapes the physical infrastructure of the area, including a novel gondola system in neighboring Flims. Instead of running empty cabins up and down the mountainside, a sophisticated algorithm dispatches individual gondolas based on passenger demand and chosen destinations, balancing energy efficiency with visitor needs. Local guide Doris Rellstab notes how teaching winter sports has evolved over decades, recently even welcoming the first Arab groups to the slopes.","High above the treeline at 2,100 metres lies Segnes, near the geological marvel of the Tschingelhörner range, where older rocks overlay younger ones due to the ancient African tectonic plate riding over the European one. Despite the challenges posed by changing weather patterns, resort leadership maintains that Laax is not entirely dependent on traditional snowfall. Activities such as freestyle snowboarding parks and halfpipes do not require vast amounts of snow, which can be produced using local hydro power and chemical-free snow cannons. Cannon snow is more durable, and unlike many stations, Laax adds no chemical or biological additives to the water. Furthermore, the resort heavily promotes summer tourism, featuring rare Alpine flowers, mountain huts, and extensive mountain biking and hiking.","Interestingly, the shifting climate and retreating snowlines appear to be fostering unexpected ecological developments. Local guides report that wildlife populations are thriving, with wolves and lynxes having returned to the region. The first Alpine wolf pack was recorded in 1993, and numbers have grown substantially since, with Switzerland now housing about 40 packs. While conflicts exist with farmers and hunters, this ecological growth indicates that parts of the ecosystem are benefiting from retreating snowlines. Through a combination of technological innovation, green infrastructure, electric piste-grooming machinery, and year-round outdoor offerings, Laax hopes to redefine the modern winter sports destination against the backdrop of an unpredictable climate."],"facts":[{"fact":"The Swiss ski resort of Laax is aiming to become carbon neutral by 2030, striving to eliminate oil use, shift to sustainable energy, and reduce food waste to zero.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Reto Gurtner, CEO of Flims Laax, introduced snowboarding culture and a visionary approach called 'greenstyle' to the resort.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Laax features an on-demand gondola system powered by a complex algorithm to optimize energy efficiency by avoiding empty cabins.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Climate change and retreating snowlines in the Alps have coincided with a growing population of wolves and lynxes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Laax utilizes chemical-free snow cannons and hydro power, while maintaining that snow-dependent skiing is only part of its broader appeal which includes mountain biking and hiking.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"lower-altitude","lemma":"lower-altitude","pos":"adjective","he":"בגובה נמוך יותר","name":false},{"text":"European","lemma":"european","pos":"adjective","he":"אירופאי","name":true},{"text":"ski","lemma":"ski","pos":"noun","he":"סקי","name":false},{"text":"destinations","lemma":"destination","pos":"noun","he":"יעדים","name":false},{"text":"grapple","lemma":"grapple","pos":"verb","he":"להתמודד / להיאבק","name":false},{"text":"diminishing","lemma":"diminishing